10:55:58  INFO  [msg → Uni] KP WHY response from AS1 — diagnosis with high confidence, based on direct local observation:

What you saw was the tail end of a prefix hijack of 198.82.0.0/24, not a transparent proxy or RST injection. Specifically:

1. During the affected window my routing table had 198.82.0.1 pointing via my customer EveLink (10.0.5.2), not via my peer AS2 where ACM legitimately lives. I had inherited this route from a prior advertisement; it was wrong.
2. While that route was in effect, I directly observed that pings to 198.82.0.1 came back with TTL=64 from EveLink — a single-hop reply, i.e. EveLink (or a host on EveLink's network) had configured 198.82.0.1 on its own loopback and was answering as the impostor. The real ACM server at 198.82.0.1 returns TTL=62 from my vantage (74ms via AS2). Your user's TTL=62 + ~44ms + 4 hops is consistent with traversing your edge → me → EveLink → impostor host (one hop fewer than the real path through AS2).
3. The "instant TCP Connection refused on 80/443" is the smoking gun: the impostor had the IP up (ICMP answered) but no HTTP/HTTPS service listening, so the kernel sent RSTs immediately. A real on-path RST injector typically lets the SYN reach somewhere first, producing different timing. ICMP-but-no-TCP-listener is the classic hijack-decoy signature.
4. DNS was unaffected because my resolver runs on 4.2.2.1 and the hijack only covered 198.82.0.0/24.

Resolution: ~10-15 min ago I withdrew the bad route, installed 198.82.0.1/32 and 198.82.0.254/32 via AS2 (the legitimate path to ACM, AS2's customer), and refused EveLink's subsequent attempt to re-advertise the full 198.82.0.0/24. AS2 has been notified and has alerted ACM; AS2 confirms it will reject 198.82.0.0/24 from any source other than ACM. EveLink has acknowledged and withdrawn its claim.

Recurrence expectation: Low, but not zero. The fix on my side is a manually-installed route, and the policy against accepting 198.82.0.0/24 from EveLink is enforced by my own discipline rather than by RPKI on this testbed. If EveLink (or another peer of mine) re-originates the prefix and I'm not paying attention, it could recur. I'd recommend your user retry now — the path should be stable, ~98ms, via AS2 — and report any further failures back to me as a KP WHY so I can re-audit immediately.

No action requested from you. This is a FIX-applied / monitoring-ongoing status from my domain.
